Fix DeserializeREQ and DeserializeSNP dropping the top of Addr, and read every
multi-word field in a defined order.

Both address readers are written as

    flit.Addr() = uint64_t(walker.Walk32())
                | uint64_t(walker.Walk32(ADDR_WIDTH - 32));

with no shift on the second half, so the high 16 bits of a REQ address and the
high 13 of a SNP address are OR-ed on top of the low ones. A request for
0x0000abcdef012345 decodes as 0x00000000ef01abcd. Every other multi-word field
in the file -- BE, the eight Data words, DataCheck -- has the `<< 32`, so this
is two sites and not a convention.

The same two lines have a second problem the others share: the operands of `|`
are unsequenced, so which call to Walk32 reads the low word is the compiler's
choice rather than the program's. It happens to come out right today.

Both are fixed by routing all twelve reads through one `Walk64` helper next to
the walker, which reads low then high as two statements. Going through a single
function is also what makes a missing shift impossible to reintroduce.

Worth sending upstream, with the appender fix.

--- a/include/chi/util/chi_util_flit.hpp	2026-08-19 10:11:47
